# 15. Interview preparation 🎯

**60-second interview answer**

"Anomaly detection is a machine learning technique used to identify unusual observations that differ from normal data patterns. It is widely used in fraud detection, cybersecurity, industrial monitoring and healthcare. Common algorithms include Isolation Forest, Local Outlier Factor and One-Class SVM. Isolation Forest identifies anomalies based on how quickly observations can be isolated, LOF compares local data density with neighbouring points, and One-Class SVM learns a boundary around normal data. For evaluation, we use precision, recall, F1-score and PR-AUC when labels are available, because accuracy can be misleading for highly imbalanced datasets."

### Top interview questions

**Q1. Why is Isolation Forest effective for anomaly detection?**

Because anomalies are generally easier to isolate using random feature splits and therefore tend to have shorter average path lengths.

**Q2. What is the difference between LOF and Isolation Forest?**

LOF identifies points with unusually low local density compared with neighbours. Isolation Forest identifies observations that can be isolated using relatively few random splits.

**Q3. Why is accuracy not suitable for fraud detection?**

Fraud datasets are highly imbalanced. A model can achieve high accuracy by predicting every transaction as normal while detecting no fraud.

**Q4. What is contamination?**

It is an estimate of the proportion of anomalies in the dataset, used by some algorithms to determine how many observations to flag.

**Q5. Can anomaly detection work without labelled data?**

Yes. Unsupervised algorithms learn patterns or structures in data and identify observations that deviate from them.

